# Former Gods, Part 10
Tamil title: மாஜி கடவுள்கள் - பகுதி 10

Grinding her teeth, the goddess Tiamat summoned the god Kingu and ordered, “War! War! Now we wage war against Ea’s horde!” Both sides began assembling armies for the Babylonian war between gods and demons. Ea and his followers were the gods; Tiamat’s company were the demons.

Urged on by Tiamat, Kingu created eleven kinds of monsters, including serpent-men, scorpion-men, and bird-men. Tiamat also fixed the Tablet of Destinies upon Kingu’s breast, giving him power to determine even the gods’ fate. Learning this, mighty Ea wailed for days: “Disaster has come, and we can find no way out!”

Ea went to Anshar to deliberate. “Yes, son, this is danger beyond endurance. What can we do?” said Anshar, and he too wept. Then he called his son Anu and pleaded: “Go to the enraged Tiamat, who has set out to destroy every one of us. Negotiate peace and avert our approaching ruin.” Obeying his father, Anu went but could not approach her. Ea attempted the same sacred mission, achieved nothing, and returned in terror.

Anshar reflected and found a plan. He summoned Ea’s divine son Marduk and asked him to undertake the task. This Babylonian tale resembles some of our myths in which Murugan possesses power that Siva lacks.

Marduk was not only strong but shrewd. “If I complete this work of destruction, what reward will I receive?” he bargained. “Ask for anything,” said the other gods. “Then, if everyone accepts me hereafter as supreme over all gods, I shall destroy the demonic host and save you,” he replied. The gods agreed.

After this ceremony in their jewelled hall, the God of gods mounted a chariot drawn by four poison-breathing horses. A mace was in his hand, his whole body blazed with fire, and lightning ran before him. Anu gave him a magic net capable of binding anyone. Thus armed for battle, Marduk departed while the gods blessed him with cries of victory. The divine warrior advanced, unleashing seven mighty winds.

At his approach Tiamat opened her mouth in astonishment. It was no ordinary mouth but seven miles wide. Seizing the opportunity, the Babylonian Murugan drove all seven winds into it. The demoness could not close her mouth while the winds churned within. Marduk raised his mace, struck a mighty blow, and felled her dead. He captured Kingu, removed the Tablet of Destinies from his breast, caught the remaining demons in Anu’s magic net, and imprisoned them. He split Tiamat’s body in two: one half became the sky and the other the earth, much as our mythologists say one half of Surapadman became a cock and the other a peacock when Murugan slew him.

Babylon today has no festival of Surasamharam, no Kanda Purana, no worship or temple for Marduk, and no glib exponents assigning philosophical meanings to the seven winds and eleven monsters. Here devotees still sing, “Praise to the lord of the cock banner! Praise to the conqueror of Sura!” They are found not only among ordinary people but even among those who have risen to the position of High Court judge.

Babylonian mythology says that in a battle more terrible than the destruction of Sura, an immensely powerful god saved his father and prevailed. Yet Babylonians rejected it as a meaningless, useless tale invented in an age of blind knowledge. Marduk became a former god.

While Marduk remained divine, he enjoyed a splendid life: beautiful temples, ablutions, worship, and offerings. Just as the “thousand-name worship” continues here, ordinary Babylonians, accepting priestly fraud as sacred narrative, worshipped him by thirty-one holy names.

“Praise to the rider of the peacock!

Praise to Valli’s bridegroom!

Praise to the destroyer of Sura!

Praise to him who instructed his father!”

Such worship continues here. In Babylon Marduk became a former god: reason prevailed, delusion died, knowledge rules, ignorance perished, and priestly fraud was expelled.

Babylonia’s primal deity was Ea, whose form was a fish. At the sacred place Eridu, people built him a marvellous temple, conducted many festivals, and worshipped him. Ea was the all-protector and omnipotent one who lived in the sea. Believing deeply that he created the whole cosmos and the first human, Babylonians raised a magnificent temple for this fish incarnation.

## The Fish Incarnation

Even now many here speak of the glory of Vishnu’s fish incarnation and treat it as worthy of worship. If one asks what mockery it is for God to become a fish, devotees flare with anger. Mythologists rebuke him: “Fool! Do you not know that Matsya is one of the Lord’s ten incarnations, assumed by the wearer of the tulasi garland to rescue the four Vedas? Do you possess the maturity to understand the secret buried in these incarnations?”

If anyone argues that incarnation stories arose from fertile minds in an age when humanity lacked explanations for natural events, orthodox eyes turn into swords. Ignorance lies there as an immovable rock.

Others possess a little clarity but much cunning. They say: “Set aside whether God truly took the fish incarnation and whether the story is fact or an allegory devised by sages to convey lofty philosophy. The world was submerged. The Vedas, the very life of human progress, had to be saved. Could God resolve that crisis as an elephant, lion, or tiger? Of course not. Thus he became a fish. With what beautiful, intelligent imagination did our divinely blessed ancestors fashion the story! Only one ignorant of ancient greatness could fail to see it. Where outside our country can people with such imaginative ornament be found?” They hope both their reputation for intellect and the old order will survive.

No researcher would dare claim the fish incarnation is the exclusive creation of our mythologists and has no counterpart elsewhere. When humanity was in its playful childhood, many countries invented similar tales and worshipped similar gods.

Do not imagine that only Mahavishnu assumed a fish form. The Babylonian mythologist describes a worldwide deluge much as ours does, and says God became a fish there too.

Ea, Babylonia’s primal fish-god, was worshipped at his splendid temple in Eridu with many festivals. Archaeologists have even identified stone portions of that temple. Ea lived in the sea and came ashore each day to summon people and teach them knowledge and rare arts, from agriculture to pottery. The fish-god gave Babylonia its laws; all knowledge came from him. Believing he made the universe and primordial humanity, the people honoured him with a magnificent temple whose steps were marble.

The fish incarnation possessed even greater glory in Babylonia than here, but only until true knowledge arose. When the ray of reason appeared, Ea departed and became a former god. The power to inquire developed, and people forgot the meaningless story. Today in Babylonia not even a madman comes forward to worship the fish incarnation. Scholars call this strange god one product of humanity’s mental delusion.

Upon learning the way of the true God and gaining clear ideas about cosmic origins and industrial development, those people refused to worship gods portrayed in pointless stories devised by idlers. They threw away old tales fraudsters had made for fools. That happened there. But here, even today, people insist that only one who believes in Matsya is a theist and whoever rejects it an atheist. Not merely the unlettered but many carrying the certificate of education say so. Babylonia discarded its fish incarnation as rotten; Bharatavarsha refuses to forget its own. There Matsya became a former god; here a minister speaking about the fisheries department begins with a myth about its glory.

In every country, before clarity arose, assemblies of gods existed in this fashion. Our mythologist says Indra changes frequently: anyone may become Indra through accumulated merit. Many Indras therefore appear in succession, but Indrani remains the same wife. It is like collectors changing while the office chair remains: Punnaivananathar sits today in the same chair used last month by Bhuvanagiri Mudaliar and earlier by Abdul Latif. Whoever becomes Indra receives the same Indrani as wife.

Similarly, Babylonian mythology said male gods might die every year while female deities possessed immortality. Today knowledge says God is one, not many. The earlier arrangement included not only many gods but male and female kinds, mortal gods and immortal gods.

When new knowledge arose, the conviction prevailed that even saying “gods” was the speech of one who did not know God. Good sense recognized that empty talk by the ignorant had set gods walking about. Those who attained clarity laughed at the plural. Perhaps realizing they had no further employment, the Babylonian gods became former gods.

Gods who had lived magnificently enthroned in temples became former gods. Rulers and warriors had bowed at their feet; devotees had offered everything from human sacrifices to jewelled ornaments; poets had sung and priests danced while they reigned in temples with boundless fame. Yet all became former. No one there worships them today by supplying philosophical interpretations. No one argues that although the stories are meaningless it would be wrong to reject them because epic polish has been applied. There, a thousand crows could not stand before one stone.

And here? Devotees remain ready to accept any newly supplied deity as one more among the ten. Anyone would call it foolish to retain an iron shackle after receiving a golden anklet. A person who believes two eyes insufficient and cuts his face to add a sore is mad. What can we call someone who wears tree bark over silk? How can those who speak the true doctrine that God is one, the abode of grace and source of love, yet stubbornly refuse to relinquish fish, boar, and tortoise forms be called true theists?

In other countries imagined gods marked by obscenity and meaninglessness became former gods. Babylonia’s fish incarnation was one of them.

## The Dushasana Who Stripped Her Garment

“Guardian of the gate! Guardian of the gate!
Come swiftly and open the door.
My lover is within; make haste and open!
Let me see him; open the door!
If you refuse, I shall not stand idle.
Know that the door will be shattered;
I shall break every ancient gate in turn.
Gatekeeper, come swiftly!
I shall raise the dead against the living
and annihilate them, know this well!
The living are few and the dead are many;
therefore open the door at once.”

Exhausted from searching everywhere for her lover, her body wasted and heart distressed, the maiden at last learned where he was. She heard that the place had restraints, venomous guards, many doors, and savage customs. Yet when she knew her lover was there she forgot everything, hastened forth, found a barred gate, and pounded upon it. When no one came, she cried to the gatekeeper with the words above.

That much alone would suffice for our connoisseurs. They would read and savour it repeatedly, separate every word, explain its meaning, and swell with delight even if their listeners did not.

We have heard only of a lover running in search of his beloved. Here is novelty: the woman wanders in search of the man, not merely through forest and hill but everywhere until she discovers his abode.

She found the place;

desire seized her;

with poetic power she voiced her longing.

Do not ask whether the maiden knew poetry. They would delight in saying that poetry flowers in love, that she was the embodiment of love, love itself. Nor does the story end with this flavour; it grows.

The gatekeeper came and saw her but did not open. He ran to report the news to his mistress.

“Is she beautiful?”

“Yes, beauty’s very limit.”

“Does she cry and writhe?”

“Yes. Even stone would melt at her lament.”

“Does she demand that the door be opened?”

“Yes. The sword-eyed woman vows to smash it if we do not.”

“Watch what I do today to one who threatens me. Do not open the gate!”

“But she is screaming!”

“That is music to me.”

“What if she stops screaming and tries to break the door?”

“Yes, that man-killer may do so. Open it and bring her here, but according to our customary honours. Let the wicked little creature learn the majesty and power of our rule.”

Thus mistress and guard spoke. He opened the gate and led the waiting maiden into the enormous mansion. It was pitch dark. Many kinds of birds circled overhead with beating wings. Horrific noises tore at the ears; dust and stench were everywhere. The queen of beauty was led through it all without concern, trusting that her beloved was there and she would see and regain him.

The guard could not disobey the order to receive her with the customary honours. What were those honours?

At the first gateway her crown was taken. At the second she lost her earrings; at the third, her necklace; at the fourth, many jewels; at the fifth, her girdle. She neither grew angry nor lamented these humiliations, but cried only, “Go on! Take me where my lover is!” At the sixth gate even her clothing was seized and the peacock-like woman stripped. “Why this deed?” she asked. “What can I do, adorned lady? It is the command of our queen Allatu,” replied the guard.

She was dragged farther and farther for a great distance, then halted before a ceremonial hall. Queen Allatu sat there majestically, and the unclothed beauty stood before her.

“See how she stands like a lioness even after so many disgraces!” Allatu taunted. “It is not enough that she has been tormented so. She wants her lover, does she? I shall give her every cruel disease!”

She summoned Namtar, an evil god who inflicted illness, and ordered him to touch the woman and fill her whole body with disease. Namtar’s destructive power did so, and the beauty began to decay.

Did her suffering end with her? No; it spread throughout the world and reproduction itself ceased. How our aesthetes would rejoice if they obtained such a story! If the beauty took so long to pass from gate to gate, our great poets would take a hundred times longer. Had her story fallen to “Emperor of Poetry” Kamban, how long would crossing the sixth threshold have taken? He would have written a hundred verses, inserted exclamations such as “Ah! What wonder! A sight rarely seen!”, kneaded them with erotic flavour, and declared that even if Hari, Hara, and Brahma came to stop him he must compose twenty more verses or lose happiness in this life and the next. Fortunately, that unclothed woman did not enter Kamban’s imagination.

Before continuing, friends should consider whether this tale seems a combination of the imagined Savitri and Draupadi. Not only did the world tremble at the evil fate of the good woman seeking her lord; the gods too grew angry, for the woman subjected to such torment and disgrace was none other than the queen of heaven, one among the many gods.

Such was the heavenly queen’s love. She wandered seeking her beloved and finally reached hell. Allatu was queen there. Out of jealousy, anger, and vengeance toward Ishtar, the queen of heaven, she stripped her and sent disease upon her. The greater gods eventually restrained this injustice and rescued the heavenly queen from hell.

Is this fantasy even slightly inferior to our ancient puranas? Who could say so? It contains abundance of all the “nine flavours” our connoisseurs seek. It too is a story of gods, not humans.

This is Babylonian mythology: Ishtar, queen of heaven, loved the divine Tammuz and entered hell to find him; treacherous Allatu humiliated and afflicted her until the primal gods rescued her.

Ishtar, Allatu, and Tammuz were not merely epic characters but gods. Babylonians built temples and worshipped them with no shortage of rites or sanctity. Now Ishtar, Tammuz, and the harmful Allatu are gone, as are their temples, festivals, chariots, processions, and sacred hymns. All became former gods.

When scholars warned the Babylonians not to ruin their minds by believing such imaginary gods, did people readily accept it? Would they? They cursed: “Fool! You disparage our ancestors’ gods, the glory of mythology, and the sanctity of worship. You will be destroyed!” Their anger eventually subsided, but the momentum of new knowledge did not. Its advocacy continued until Ishtar, Allatu, and the rest departed from the people’s thought and even mention of them prompted the amused reply that they were products of remote ancestors’ intellectual confusion.

There too, as here, such tales appeared contrary to reason when taken literally. Some tried claiming that a profound meaning emerged if Ishtar represented power, Tammuz pleasure, and Allatu sorrow. The young laughed: “We can understand the philosophy that power enables one to wipe away sorrow and attain pleasure plainly enough. Why drag us into a six-gated palace, stand a naked beauty before us, and show us a hideous god?”

With time these “deities” lost esteem and people sought true knowledge. That long journey has brought much of the world to worship of one God. Here people argue that thirty-three crores of gods must exist. When obscenities in their stories are exposed, faces and noses redden with anger. Why? Many mistakenly treat condemnation of inventions as a wicked attack on belief in God. They ask whether it is necessary and imagine that bitterness toward God makes us write this way. These are needless accusations built upon a false assumption.

In countless countries people treated innumerable gods as objects of worship, conducted many festivals, and engaged in frenzy. Deep conviction and fear of ruin in this world and the next made them cling to custom. If doubt arose, they drove away the “devil of doubt” and sought a sacred teacher’s feet. But new knowledge struck with enough force to shatter both fear and longing. Though antiquity resisted stubbornly and fought with terrible weapons, it was defeated. Before defeat it threatened people with hymns, spells, jewelled garlands, skulls, cremation-ground earth, and countless other things.

Before the light of knowledge, many deities formed by ancient imagination vanished without leaving an address. Today even elders in those countries, not merely the young, do not know their old gods’ names. Once, deities thought so mighty that they demanded even emperors’ daughters as sacrifices sat amid priests whose pointing fingers served as sceptres, beneath sky-high towers and radiant halls. Today they cannot be found however long one searches.

This did not cause ignorance and atheism to run riot there, nor words of grace to die under delusion. Those countries remain in a better condition than ours and were not destroyed.

To construct a multistorey house, builders first use timber or a mound of earth, but their method changes as each level rises. Once complete, they do not retain the initial timber or earth heap; they remove it and walk upstairs. When grain ripens, farmers do not refuse harvest because the plant produced it. They tread, thresh, and beat the plant that yielded red rice.

Harvest in the world of ideas is the same. Those who do not understand grow weary and clamour, yet even they can learn that this method has occurred across the world: it is neither new nor wicked nor the conduct of fools. In ancient times, people in many lands possessed numerous deities fashioned with imagination and epic beauty like those created here, and even sceptred rulers prostrated before them. But when new knowledge...

---NOTES---
This installment begins mid-narrative with Tiamat’s declaration of war, continuing the Babylonian creation account from `maji_kadavulgal9.md`. It ends mid-sentence after `புத்தறிவு`; `maji_kadavulgal11.md` continues it with `பிறந்ததும்...` (“...was born”).

The source uses older Tamil forms/transliterations for Babylonian figures, including `டியாமட்` (Tiamat), `கிங்கு` (Kingu), `ஈயா` (Ea), `அன்ஷார்` (Anshar), `அனு` (Anu), `மெரோடாக்` (Marduk), `இஷ்டார்` (Ishtar), `டாமூஜ்` (Tammuz), `அலாட்டூ` (Allatu), and `நாம்டார்` (Namtar). Conventional English spellings are used without altering the verbatim Tamil source.

The OCR contains isolated damaged strings (`பகட்க`, a stray line after Image 2, and other typographical debris). They remain untouched in the source block and are not invented into the translation.

`குக்குடம்` is glossed by the printed footnote as “cock”; the translation therefore renders `குக்குடக் கொடி` as “cock banner.”
